use warnings;
use strict;

=head1 NAME

linux_entity - snmpd pass_persist for ENTITY-MIB and ENTITY-SENSOR-MIB transceiver info on Linux

=head1 VERSION

0.1.0

=cut

our $VERSION = '0.1.0';

=head1 SYNOPSIS

linux_entity B<-u> [B<-d> <cache dir>] [B<-i> <regexp>]... [B<-I> <regexp>]...

linux_entity B<-p> <base OID> [B<-d> <cache dir>]

linux_entity B<-w> [B<-d> <cache dir>]

=head1 DESCRIPTION

Reports pluggable transceivers, SFP, QSFP, and the like, as found via
C<ethtool -m>, as ENTITY-MIB inventory and their digital optical monitoring
readings as ENTITY-SENSOR-MIB sensors.

It works in two parts. Ran with B<-u> from cron as root, it reads the
transceivers and writes the cache. Ran with B<-p> by snmpd, it answers
requests from the cache. snmpd never waits on ethtool, which can take
seconds per port on some drivers and needs root.

=head1 SNMPD CONFIG

    # cron
    */5 * * * * root /etc/snmp/extends/linux_entity -u

    # snmpd.conf
    pass_persist .1.3.6.1.2.1.47 /etc/snmp/extends/linux_entity -p .1.3.6.1.2.1.47
    pass_persist .1.3.6.1.2.1.99 /etc/snmp/extends/linux_entity -p .1.3.6.1.2.1.99

Two lines are needed as the MIBs are in separate trees. B<-p> is told the
tree as snmpd does not pass along what it was registered for.

Run B<-u> once before restarting snmpd so the cache exists. Without a
cache, nothing is returned.

Check that snmpd does not already answer for ENTITY-MIB first, as some
builds might. This should return nothing...

    snmpwalk -v2c -c public localhost .1.3.6.1.2.1.47

=head1 REQUIREMENTS

ethtool, plus a NIC and driver that support reading the module EEPROM.
Not all do. Readings only show up for modules that support digital
optical monitoring.

=head1 FLAGS

=head2 -u

Update the cache. Needs root, as C<ethtool -m> does.

=head2 -p <base OID>

Run as a snmpd pass_persist for the tree under the base OID.

=head2 -w

Print the cache the same as snmpwalk would, for debugging.

=head2 -d <cache dir>

The cache directory. Defaults to /var/cache/linux_entity. The cache is
entity.json in it.

=head2 -i <regexp>

Only check interfaces with a name matching this regexp. May be used more
than once, in which case an interface matching any of them is checked.

=head2 -I <regexp>

Skip interfaces with a name matching this regexp. May be used more than
once. Checked after B<-i>.

Only interfaces with a device under /sys/class/net/$interface/device are
checked to begin with, so virtual ones such as bridges, veth, and VLANs
are already skipped.

=head1 ENTITIES

Each port with a readable module gets a port entity, a module entity under
it, and a sensor entity for each reading under that. Indexes are derived
from the kernel ifindex so they stay the same between runs.

- 1 :: The chassis, using what is in /sys/class/dmi/id.

- $ifindex * 1000 :: The port. entAliasMappingIdentifier maps it to
  IF-MIB::ifIndex.$ifindex.

- $ifindex * 1000 + 1 :: The module. Vendor name, part number, revision,
  and serial go in entPhysicalMfgName, ModelName, HardwareRev, and SerialNum.

- $ifindex * 1000 + 10 :: Module temperature, celsius.

- $ifindex * 1000 + 11 :: Module voltage, voltsDC.

- $ifindex * 1000 + 100 + $lane :: Laser bias current, amperes at milli scale.

- $ifindex * 1000 + 200 + $lane :: Tx power, other with dBm as the units.

- $ifindex * 1000 + 300 + $lane :: Rx power, other with dBm as the units.

$lane is 0 for single lane modules and the channel number for multi lane
ones, such as 1 to 4 for QSFP.

The Tx and Rx power sensors are named "$interface Tx Power Sensor" and
"$interface Rx Power Sensor", or "$interface Lane $lane Rx Power Sensor"
and so on for multi lane modules. ENTITY-SENSOR-MIB lacks a dBm type and
LibreNMS uses those names to treat them as dBm. A power reading of zero,
shown by ethtool as -inf dBm, is reported as -40 dBm.

Alarm and warning thresholds are not reported as ENTITY-SENSOR-MIB has
nowhere to put them.

Interfaces with a ifindex over 2147482 are skipped, as the indexes would
not fit in a entPhysicalIndex.

=head1 INSTALL

Debian...

    apt-get install ethtool libjson-perl libfile-slurp-perl

Generic...

    cpanm JSON File::Slurp

=cut

